{"review_id": "iclr_nLlBLzPpeG_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_nLlBLzPpeG", "passages": [{"quote": "The idea is well explained and simple to understand.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "clarity of explanation"}, {"quote": "It make sense to try to leverage the additional diversity we have in the majority groups to augment the number of classes in the minority groups.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "conceptual soundness"}, {"quote": "Authors show good improvements over the DAFusion baseline.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "empirical performance"}, {"quote": "I appreciate that the authors took the time to describe their preliminary results that did not work in the appendix.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "transparency in reporting"}, {"quote": "Lack of relevant related works. The authors claim that they are the first to study automated data augmentation with generative models which clearly show they are not aware that there is an entire research field on this question.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "related work coverage"}, {"quote": "This lack of relevant related work induce a lack of relevant baselines. I would have expected to see comparisons between AutoGenDA and methods like Fill-Up, Diffuse-Mix or the others.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline completeness"}, {"quote": "Lack of novelty. If we look at the works I just listed, I am not convinced that the method presented by the authors is any better than the current existing methods for data augmentation using synthetic data.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty"}, {"quote": "Authors did not use any of the traditional datasets use to evaluate imbalance classification (like CIFAR-10-LT, CIFAR100-LT, Places-LT ,ImageNet-LT, Inat18, NICO++).", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "dataset selection"}, {"quote": "The ablation study does not show for now statistically significant improvement by using AutoGenDA (~0.5% d'accuracy and the authors does not present what is the variance or the dataset they are using for the ablation).", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "statistical significance"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_nLlBLzPpeG_r4", "paper_id": "iclr_nLlBLzPpeG", "passages": [{"quote": "The problem of learning from long-tailed data is important in practice.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem importance"}, {"quote": "The idea is natural.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "conceptual naturalness"}, {"quote": "The basic assumption may be not fully correct. I think the text-guided image-to-image generator should also be trained with long-tailed data.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "assumption validity"}, {"quote": "Experiments are not sufficient. Results on ImageNet-LT and iNaturalist should be provided.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental completeness"}, {"quote": "Many important baselines are not considered.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline completeness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4", "passages": [{"quote": "The problems this paper tries to address are spot on, current VLMs is limited in terms of complex visual reasoning, natural interaction with human, etc.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem relevance"}, {"quote": "The method to evolve current instruction fine-tune data to enhance these capabilities is one of interesting direction to improve the model's capabilities, as shown in the benchmark results.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "methodological direction"}, {"quote": "The authors mention data and code will be released, and I think it'll be a good contribution to the community to set a higher baseline for LLAVA style model and maybe even beyond.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "community contribution"}, {"quote": "My major concern is lack of technical clarity and minor concern is insufficient evaluation.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "technical clarity and evaluation"}, {"quote": "can you explictly describe what model, commercial API or other method is used to generate the evolutoin instruction data from the prompt templates?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "methodological specification"}, {"quote": "it is mentioned pseudo function calling is used for visual reasoning evolution, can you describe the setup of function call", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "technical detail completeness"}, {"quote": "it is mentioned MLLM is used for rewriting, can you describe details on what MLLM is used, how it is used (prompt used), evalution or abalation of the significance of this rewriting step?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "rewriting methodology detail"}, {"quote": "while there is few OCR related benchmark results, more results on OCRBench, ChratQA, DocVQA, TextVQA would be very insightful.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "OCR benchmark coverage"}, {"quote": "more ablation on ratio of the three evolution methods in each round, how to decide and eliminate failed evolution, what's the success/fail ratio for each round, what is the model quality gain for reach round, etc. would be informative.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "ablation study depth"}, {"quote": "InternVL2-8b (releaesd 2024/07) and Qwen2-VL-7b (released 2024/08) should be included in Table2 under weight open source section.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline currency"}]}
{"review_id": "ANCH-2", "paper_id": "ANCH-2", "passages": [{"quote": "This submission provides a rigorous theoretical analysis; the proofs appear correct and the assumptions are clearly stated.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "theoretical rigor"}, {"quote": "My main concern is reproducibility: no code is provided and several hyperparameters are unreported.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "reproducibility"}, {"quote": "The related-work coverage is adequate.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "related work coverage"}, {"quote": "Overall the significance for the broader community seems modest.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "broader significance"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper addresses the issue of data scarcity in instruction datasets for training multimodal large language models, presenting a well-motivated problem.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem motivation"}, {"quote": "The authors designed a sophisticated pipeline to enhance data quality, featuring three evolution stages and an instruction elimination stage.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "pipeline sophistication"}, {"quote": "The authors evaluate the effectiveness of the three proposed evolution stages, as shown in Table 1.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "ablation study presence"}, {"quote": "The performance of **MMEval** MLLMs in Table 2 appears promising.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "empirical performance"}, {"quote": "The entire data improvement framework relies on closed-source frontier models like GPT-4, which suggests a form of knowledge distillation from these models but may limit the ability to scale to larger datasets.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "scalability limitation"}, {"quote": "the strong dependence on models like GPT-4 reduces the framework's interpretability.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "interpretability"}, {"quote": "The authors do not provide an analysis of the reliability of using GPT-4 as a data rewriter.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "rewriter reliability analysis"}, {"quote": "The paper lacks a comparison of different prompts used in each evolution stage, leaving the impact of prompt templates on data refinement unclear.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "prompt template analysis"}, {"quote": "although **MMEval-8B** shows overall improvements, it exhibits significant performance declines on key benchmarks like **MMMU**, **AI2D**, and **MMStar**.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "benchmark-specific degradation"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4", "passages": [{"quote": "the authors propose MMEvol, a novel multimodal instruction data evolution framework that augments existing multi-modal training data with better diversity and complexity.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "data augmentation approach"}, {"quote": "The experiment results show that the proposed method works well and helps the MLLMs get better performance", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "empirical effectiveness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4_r4", "paper_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4", "passages": [{"quote": "This work proposes an image-text instruction evolution framework, Evol-Instruct, to enhance the quality and quantity of instruction data.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "framework contribution"}, {"quote": "Using three distinct evolution methods and instruction elimination to remove harmful data, the approach increases the complexity and diversity of a limited seed dataset.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "data quality enhancement"}, {"quote": "After three rounds of evolution, the resulting data trains a new model that achieves SOTA performance across various benchmarks.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "state-of-the-art performance"}, {"quote": "Evolving multimodal dataset makes sense and is so interesting but actual performnace improvements are too marginal in my perspective: 2~3% because evaluating language ability can improve large margin if it is really contrbutional approach.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "performance improvement magnitude"}, {"quote": "Experiments should be compared for fair comparison where same architecture, same dataset to provide the effectiveness of MMEvol.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental fairness"}, {"quote": "What kind of dataset samples are more effective to be applited by MMEvol like Math, code, or anyhting?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "applicability analysis"}]}
{"review_id": "ANCH-1", "paper_id": "ANCH-1", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper tackles an interesting problem.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem relevance"}, {"quote": "the novelty is limited: the proposed method is a straightforward combination of two existing techniques.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty"}, {"quote": "The writing is also difficult to follow in Sections 3-4, with undefined notation.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing clarity"}, {"quote": "On the positive side, the experimental evaluation is thorough, covering five datasets with proper ablations.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental thoroughness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4_r5", "paper_id": "iclr_E0dTlxy1T4", "passages": [{"quote": "The MMEvol framework effectively enhances the diversity and complexity of image-text instruction data through iterative evolution methods", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "data enhancement effectiveness"}, {"quote": "This approach significantly improves the quality of data used for training MLLMs, addressing a critical bottleneck in existing data-driven methods.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "bottleneck resolution"}, {"quote": "The proposed method achieves an average accuracy improvement of 3.1 percentage points over baseline models and reaches state-of-the-art performance in nine vision-language tasks.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "empirical performance"}, {"quote": "The generated instructions are more compositional, longer in reasoning chains, and more balanced between objects. The statistics of the instructions suggest improved quality of instructions.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "instruction quality improvement"}, {"quote": "While the paper claims that the instruction generation method is a key contribution, it lacks a direct comparison with other multimodal instruction generation techniques, such as MIMIC-IT or LLaVA-NEXT.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline comparison completeness"}, {"quote": "The paper does not sufficiently explore the limitations or failure scenarios of MMEvol.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "failure case analysis"}, {"quote": "The paper's technical contribution feels incremental compared to previous works like MIMIC-IT[1] or MM-Instruct[2], which also iteratively refine instructions from image metadata.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "technical novelty"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_SIzjhS9kEF_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_SIzjhS9kEF", "passages": [{"quote": "This work presents in-depth analyses, offering insights for understanding the post-training process.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "analytical depth"}, {"quote": "It finds that the format-related errors are often saturated with a few samples, but the reasoning ability requries much more examples to improve.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "insight specificity"}, {"quote": "the choice of task is limited by the size of the training set. However, the training set is still small for building a reliable scaling law.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "training set size"}, {"quote": "the main findings (the key takeaways section) are not a surprise; some of them have been discussed in the community. The contribution of this work is more like to be a verficiation and provide more evidence.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty of findings"}, {"quote": "this work uses the term \"post-training\" frequently, but the actual experiments are limited to SFT rather than RLHF.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "scope of experiments"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_SIzjhS9kEF_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_SIzjhS9kEF", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper conducts a comprehensive evaluation using well-defined benchmarks,  providing evidence that post-training improves more than stylistic alignment.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "evaluation comprehensiveness"}, {"quote": "by analyzing the power-law relationship in post-training, it shows that model improvements align well with scaling laws observed in pre-training.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "scaling law insight"}, {"quote": "Using GPT-based evaluation for alignment and error analysis introduces potential biases.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "evaluation methodology bias"}, {"quote": "Missing Citation: there are some related work that also demonstrate the scaling law in the post-training stage", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "citation completeness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_SIzjhS9kEF_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_SIzjhS9kEF", "passages": [{"quote": "This paper aims to revisit the superficial alignment hypothesis, which posits that post-training primarily concerns style and format adjustments, while most capabilities and knowledge are learned during pre-training.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "research goal"}, {"quote": "The authors undertake comprehensive experiments to explore the scaling law in post-training for various reasoning abilities, thereby illuminating a feasible pathway for enhancing the advanced capabilities of large models.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental comprehensiveness"}, {"quote": "The superficial alignment hypothesis has not gained widespread acceptance in practical LLM pre-training.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "hypothesis acceptance"}, {"quote": "Similar conclusions have already been observed in existing technical reports or papers (e.g., the Figure 2 of paper [2]). This suggests that the re-evaluation presented in the paper may not be as novel as claimed.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty claim"}, {"quote": "work [2] delves into how each ability varies due to the composition of supervised fine-tuning (SFT) datasets. The authors investigate this using varying amounts of mixed data and compare the results with individual ability performance, as illustrated in the Figure 3. However, the current submission only mentions multi-task settings as a potential future direction.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "multi-task investigation depth"}, {"quote": "The distinction between pre-training and post-training has become increasingly blurred, especially with the widespread adoption of synthetic data.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "conceptual clarity"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_SIzjhS9kEF_r4", "paper_id": "iclr_SIzjhS9kEF", "passages": [{"quote": "the work study the scaling factors in post-training alignment to better counter-argue superficial alignment hypothesis. This seems a novel thing that other people haven't broadly tried.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "research direction novelty"}, {"quote": "the author basically revise the SAH to be conditional on the what it really takes to accomplish some downstream tasks. If the downstream tasks don't require more knowledge, then the alignment is only style-wise. This is a good message, although a bit obvious.", "polarity": "mixed", "aspect": "conceptual contribution"}, {"quote": "showing that win-rate and task accuracy differs is an interesting observation.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "metric insight"}, {"quote": "error analysis does seems good and comprehensive;", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "error analysis quality"}, {"quote": "Missing URIAL prompt [1] as a training-free alignment baseline, since the model is trained on LIMA-1k, it adds some complexity.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline completeness"}, {"quote": "The authors should also make the investigation/claim a bit more systematic or formal. In terms of capability X, how does superficial alignment hypothesis not hold (+ some analysis on error), how does the observation in capability  X different from observation in capability Y. I think the problem statement is not so well-stated.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "systematicity of investigation"}, {"quote": "an alternative explanation is that the model is getting there? Also, how does the overall accuracy changes? Are the number of incorrect responses decreasing? I don't think the author could conclude much here.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "claim justification"}, {"quote": "The author also didn't do thorough survey for related work. For example, [2] also study the same topic (maybe not explicitly mentioning \"superficial alignment hypothesis\") in a more thorough and focused manner.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "related work thoroughness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_5zjsZiYEnr_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_5zjsZiYEnr", "passages": [{"quote": "Research problem is challenging and necessary for this direction and various domain applications.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem importance"}, {"quote": "A new dataset is proposed for multimodal long document understanding.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "dataset contribution"}, {"quote": "A LLM-based auto-evaluating framework is introduced.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "evaluation framework"}, {"quote": "The scope of this paper limited the question only focusing on specific pages ignoring the more natural cases of answers distributed spanning pages.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "task scope limitation"}, {"quote": "the author mentioned the in-depth but there is no supporting analysis and results to show why the dataset shows more in-depth.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "claim substantiation"}, {"quote": "The dataset generation workflow uses off-the-shelf tools and models to extract the document structure which should be verified as the accumulated errors may occur when moving to the automatic QA generation stage.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "error propagation risk"}, {"quote": "More dataset analyses are expected including question length and focusing topics. Simple statistics can not show more insight of your datasets.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "dataset analysis depth"}, {"quote": "The proposed evaluation metrics may need more detailed analysis to show robustness. Current average weighting looks too simple ignoring the difference between specific models dealing with specific types of questions.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "evaluation metric robustness"}, {"quote": "As the metrics are unexplored the results may not be comprehensive and reliable. Lack of quantitative analysis show different domain, question types performance.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "result reliability"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_5zjsZiYEnr_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_5zjsZiYEnr", "passages": [{"quote": "Useful new eval dataset, training dataset, eval framework and interesting model for multimodal RAG-QA on long docs.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "contribution utility"}, {"quote": "This paper doesn't really have any major weaknesses.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "overall quality"}, {"quote": "the paper presents its contribution as being primarily a dataset paper, so there's understandably not much novelty with the models.", "polarity": "mixed", "aspect": "model novelty"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_5zjsZiYEnr_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_5zjsZiYEnr", "passages": [{"quote": "M-LongDoc is a novel benchmark dataset to evaluating multimodal long document (210 pages in average) understanding.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "benchmark novelty"}, {"quote": "M-LongDoc features 851 samples that challenge existing multimodal models / systems to answer open-ended questions requiring in-depth understanding of complex documents", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "dataset difficulty"}, {"quote": "The paper proposes a retrieval-aware tuning to improve multimodal model performance on M-LongDoc benchmarks, a strategy that could benefit applications requiring nuanced document comprehension.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "method applicability"}, {"quote": "All questions in the benchmark are synthetically generated by multimodal LLMs, which may limit the benchmark's reflection of real-world scenarios. Human annotations are not involved in the benchmark creation process.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "data generation realism"}, {"quote": "The dataset's scale is relatively modest (only 852 samples), potentially insufficient for capturing a wide range of perspectives and real-world scenarios.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "dataset scale"}, {"quote": "Evaluation relies on proprietary LLMs, introducing potential variability due to different checkpoints or versions.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "evaluation reproducibility"}, {"quote": "Some related works are missing [1], the differences are not discussed.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "related work completeness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_PNiqWDAtPq_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_PNiqWDAtPq", "passages": [{"quote": "The motivation for removing the triplet is very meaningful and reasonable in instruction-based image editing. The bias of training data is also an important problem.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem motivation"}, {"quote": "The presentation is easy to understand and the discussion for the motivation is reasonable and convincing.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "presentation clarity"}, {"quote": "There are several experiment evaluation types including quantitative, qualitative, and user study.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "evaluation diversity"}, {"quote": "Considering the diffusion process InsP2P includes multiple steps to get the final image, what is the backpropagation process of the loss constructed on the image? I think this will be a recursive procedure since the loss will have to backpropagate from the last step of diffusion to the very first step which is insanely computationally expensive and requires large GPU memory.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "computational efficiency"}, {"quote": "I think the proposed method can maintain the consistency of forward and reversed edits but cannot guarantee that the edited results are good, disentangled, and do not include any bias from the training data.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "quality guarantee"}, {"quote": "The table 4 does not clearly show this.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "ablation clarity"}, {"quote": "I think the CLIP similarity loss makes the model have the editing ability. However, this does not indicate the model can preserve the structure since the edited image can be very different from the original but have the same semantics and be edited back to the original.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "structure preservation justification"}, {"quote": "To validate the editing ability, I think the author should evaluate more diverse editing types and datasets such as PIE.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "evaluation comprehensiveness"}, {"quote": "I am also interested in the attention map visualization in forward and reversed editing. I think the attention can be different at the same timestep since the forward and reverse process are opposite.  Which attention map at which timestep is enforced with consistency? More clarifications and visualization evidence on the loss should be provided.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "methodological clarity"}, {"quote": "the equations are not numbered.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "formatting quality"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_PNiqWDAtPq_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_PNiqWDAtPq", "passages": [{"quote": "This paper is well written and easy to follow.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing quality"}, {"quote": "The task is interesting and the problem setting is reasonable.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "task relevance"}, {"quote": "Although the idea of cycle consistency has been seen in CycleGAN, proving that it can be successfully applied to instruction-based image editing also represents a certain contribution.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "adaptation contribution"}, {"quote": "Due to the lack of GroundTruth, it is difficult to make an accurate comparison of the experimental results.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "evaluation difficulty"}, {"quote": "Looking at the edited image results, the proposed method does not seem to have a clearly superior advantage compared to the methods against which it was benchmarked.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "comparative performance"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_PNiqWDAtPq_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_PNiqWDAtPq", "passages": [{"quote": "Editing the images via instruction is natural. The dataset collection is expensive. This method provides an unsupervised method to achieve this goal and several loss functions are utilized to make it happen.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "unsupervised approach value"}, {"quote": "The experiment results are detailed. Different datasets are trained to show the differences. Single-turn and multi-turn editing are considered.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental detail"}, {"quote": "This method involves several loss functions for training, which might make it hard to find the best checkpoint.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "training complexity"}, {"quote": "There are many loss functions in UIP2P. However, the author only does the ablations on the L_sim and  L_attn. What about other loss functions?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "ablation completeness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_WK6K1FMEQ1_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_WK6K1FMEQ1", "passages": [{"quote": "It is interesting to test both VLM and LLM models'  large scale 2D and 3D spatial reasoning abilities using three scenarios: Ego Image/ Video, Bird's Eye View (BEV) images, and BEV text.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "evaluation design"}, {"quote": "It also allows for the assessment of models across different modalities, including textual descriptions, single images, and video content. This design reflects the complexity of real-world spatial understanding and demonstrates the comprehensive nature of the evaluation.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "modality coverage"}, {"quote": "This article uses interactive tasks for route retracing and shortcut searching where the model receives the current observation, decides which action to take, and receives corresponding updated observations", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "task interactivity"}, {"quote": "Providing specific details such as the resolution of the videos and images used in the experiments would be better because models might take in different sizes of input images, therefore model performance can vary significantly based on input resolution.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental specification"}, {"quote": "if an image is downsampled incorrectly, it may disrupt the original aspect ratio, potentially impairing the performance in tasks that require precise spatial reasoning, such as distance and direction estimation.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "preprocessing validity"}, {"quote": "Only two VLMs are tested for image relevant tasks. And those two models even belong to the same family (GPT). I would recommend to test more SOTA VLMs to validate your conclusions.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "model diversity"}, {"quote": "the images used for experiments might be too small to draw a reliable conclusion.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "sample size"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_WK6K1FMEQ1_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_WK6K1FMEQ1", "passages": [{"quote": "The proposed benchmarks are very extensive, covering different types of spatial cognition with many tasks and input modalities.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "benchmark extensiveness"}, {"quote": "The paper is well written and many details are provided on the evaluation methodology, including detailed prompts and model settings.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "methodological detail"}, {"quote": "The topic investigated in this paper is an important one, with many implications for real-world use of foundation models.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "topic importance"}, {"quote": "there are a number of previous works that address very similar questions, and the results are not particularly surprising.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty and surprise"}, {"quote": "Both Valmeekam et al. (2023) and Momennejad et al. (2023) have shown that LLMs are very poor at navigation and planning tasks (i.e., large-scale spatial cognition), and studies from both Yamada et al. (2024) and Ivanova et al. (2024) and have already shown that LLMs have a limited ability to reason about spatial relations. These works are not cited, and there is no discussion of how the proposed benchmark goes beyond previous studies of spatial reasoning in these systems.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "related work coverage and positioning"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_WK6K1FMEQ1_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_WK6K1FMEQ1", "passages": [{"quote": "Very timely topic.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "topic timeliness"}, {"quote": "It is a quite impressive set of tasks that really engaged with the literature on spatial reasoning. The translation of spatial reasoning tasks to text only is an especially nice contribution, because it allows for the evaluation on text-only models.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "task design and literature engagement"}, {"quote": "I think the benchmark will be immediately valuable to the community.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "community value"}, {"quote": "Comprehensive evaluation. Lots of models were used, which really gives us an idea as to how the current landscape of models performs at tasks like these.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "evaluation comprehensiveness"}, {"quote": "The authors took the best first step in evaluating a capability that humans have (spatial cognition), which is to directly take tasks that have been used in cognitive science to test this capability in humans. However, these are *tests that were designed for humans specifically*, not Large (vision)-Language Models.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "task ecological validity"}, {"quote": "I think more work will need to be done in the future to *ensure these kinds of tasks are presented to the model in a way that is ecological.*", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "ecological presentation"}, {"quote": "There are a very large number of tasks and each of them are described independently. However, there isn't much discussion on what features are shared across the different tasks.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "feature abstraction"}, {"quote": "I think the paper would be a lot more stronger if the authors were able to pick out specific features of spatial cognition that are salient across many of these tasks and identify which tasks test which features.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "capability decomposition"}, {"quote": "Currently the shear number of tasks (while impressive) is overwhelming, and looking at performance across all of them is harder to interpret.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "interpretability"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_WK6K1FMEQ1_r4", "paper_id": "iclr_WK6K1FMEQ1", "passages": [{"quote": "While there's an increasing interest in spatial cognition for frontier models, there are not enough datasets to evaluate them systematically. The proposed benchmark SPACE is novel in terms of its comprehensiveness and diversity in tasks.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "benchmark novelty"}, {"quote": "SPACE is a very comprehensive and diverse benchmark. The large-scale spatial cognition tasks cover 5 tasks. The small-scale spatial cognition tasks cover 10 tasks. All 15 tasks are based on prior studies in cognitive science, which ensures plausibility in psychology and cognitive science domains.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "benchmark comprehensiveness"}, {"quote": "The input of the tests can be pure language or multimodal, which makes the benchmark suitable for evaluation on LLMs and VLMs. All experiments have human performance as the reference, which makes it easier to compare frontier models with humans.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "benchmark design quality"}, {"quote": "The paper is well-written and easy to follow. Figures are helpful for understanding each task. The gray coloring of the results indicating they are below 50% of human performance makes the results very interpretable.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "presentation clarity"}, {"quote": "This paper is valuable to the research community of large language models since it reveals the shortcoming of basic spatial cognition for these frontier models.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "research significance"}, {"quote": "Most results are around chance level accuracy. There are two possible conclusions: 1. Models have weak spatial cognition. 2. Frontier models have better spatial cognition internally, but it doesn't show externally because of the unfamiliarization of the multimodal and text-only prompt formats.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "result interpretation ambiguity"}, {"quote": "It would be more rigorous if there are some experiments show that these formats of prompting can be understood by these frontier models", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "prompt format validation"}, {"quote": "Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting techniques [1] are known for improving language model in solving tasks as it allows LLMs to have some reasoning steps before arriving at the final answer. CoT techniques are not mentioned in the main paper, except \"Think step by step\" prompts in the appendix, but it is unclear which tasks use the CoT, which doesn't.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "CoT usage clarity"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_sLKDbuyq99_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_sLKDbuyq99", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper adds value to agent-based workflow generation and automation.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "contribution value"}, {"quote": "Activity-on-vertex graphs are incorporated to better manage task dependency and parallelism.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "graph representation utility"}, {"quote": "Comparative analysis is conducted with multiple related work in terms of both success rate and human rating.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "comparative evaluation"}, {"quote": "A framework architecture or sequence diagram is needed to demonstrate the overview of the proposed solution.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "architectural documentation"}, {"quote": "I am wondering why the proposed solution does not check the workflow correctness in the first place, but the parallelism and modularity.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "workflow validation priority"}, {"quote": "Section 4.1.3: Why "Flow gets 100% success rate" while in Table 3 the overall success rate is 80%?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "result consistency"}, {"quote": "The evaluation results can be elaborated. For instance, in the three scenarios, how many updates are required respectively?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "evaluation detail"}, {"quote": "Table 1: "task 1, 2 completed, task 3 under-work", task 2 and 3 should be exchanged according to the above AOV.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "correctness"}, {"quote": "The paper organization can be adjusted. For instance, there is no Section 4.2, then why Section 4.1 is needed?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "paper organization"}, {"quote": "A proof-reading is needed as some typos are found.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing quality"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_sLKDbuyq99_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_sLKDbuyq99", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper effectively points out a significant gap in existing frameworks, specifically regarding the adaptability of workflows in real-time, especially in the face of unforeseen challenges.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "gap identification"}, {"quote": "The paper is well-structured and easy to follow, offering clear definitions and detailed formulas that enhance comprehension.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "paper structure"}, {"quote": "While dynamic workflow generation and execution have been extensively discussed in prior studies (e.g., [1][2][3][4]), this paper's approach lacks sufficient novelty.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty"}, {"quote": "Directed acyclic graphs (DAGs) and graph-based frameworks have already been established as effective structures in LLM-based agent frameworks. To strengthen its contribution, the paper should include comparative analysis with these existing approaches, highlighting specific advantages and unique aspects of the proposed method.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "comparative analysis depth"}, {"quote": "What's the cost of the Flow?  There is no discussion of Flow's cost relative to other frameworks, leaving the efficiency of the proposed method unclear.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "cost analysis"}, {"quote": "Important experimental settings, such as the number of candidate graphs (𝐺) and the agent count, are not specified.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental specification"}, {"quote": "The description of workflow refinement process lacks critical details.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "refinement process detail"}, {"quote": "Lacks several key information of Flow deisgn and execution. 1) How Flow determines when all prerequisite tasks has been completed? 2) How Flow handle the cases where some parallel tasks have not yet finished", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "execution mechanism clarity"}, {"quote": "The current experiments cover only three tasks, which may not be sufficient to substantiate Flow's superior performance comprehensively.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental scope"}, {"quote": "The paper does not address how Flow would perform with less powerful LLMs, such as GPT-3.5 or other open-source models.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "model generalization"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_sLKDbuyq99_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_sLKDbuyq99", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper presents an interesting and intuitive approach to representing workflows and enabling multi-agent systems to execute complex tasks.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "approach intuitiveness"}, {"quote": "Results support the validity of the approach.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "result validity"}, {"quote": "The paper also tackles an important open problem in the literature: automating complex tasks.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem importance"}, {"quote": "The authors also perform a good analysis of the experiments, taking the time to specify errors that different agents performed in completing their tasks.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "error analysis quality"}, {"quote": "The authors mostly evaluate their approach anecdotally as opposed to on standard benchmarks or datasets.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "benchmark usage"}, {"quote": "The paper contains grammar mistakes and presentation issues that make the paper difficult to read.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing quality"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_sLKDbuyq99_r4", "paper_id": "iclr_sLKDbuyq99", "passages": [{"quote": "This paper introduces dynamic workflow updating and modularization, which appear to be novel.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "methodological novelty"}, {"quote": "This paper clearly outlines the methodological innovations, with well-structured sections on each component, from workflow initialization to modular design.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "methodological clarity"}, {"quote": "This paper has clear contribution to the design of multi-agent collaboration frameworks, particularly for complex adaptive tasks. By improving modularity and enabling efficient concurrent sub-task execution, the proposed framework has potential applications across fields requiring adaptable, scalable automated task execution.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "contribution clarity and applicability"}, {"quote": "The general idea of supporting dynamic workflow management has been studied previously, in particular in the context of single-agent and multi-agent planning systems. It might be novel to expand this idea in LLM-based multi-agent systems, however the corresponding technical novelty may need to be clearly highlighted and better justified.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "technical novelty justification"}, {"quote": "It seems that a key assumption of this paper is that a highly modular workflow structure can reliably cope with task failures. While this is intuitively meaningful, what happens if we find that a module is unsolvable?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "assumption robustness"}, {"quote": "it is essential to theoretically analyze this assumption and understand the specific conditions for it to be valid for practical applications.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "theoretical analysis depth"}, {"quote": "If building a modular workflow is motivated by the goal of improving robustness of workflow execution, why didn't authors directly analyze the expected amount of changes required by any workflow due to unexpected/random task failures?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "robustness metric choice"}, {"quote": "The discussion of how subtasks are assigned to different agents with varied roles is quite brief. The lack of sufficient technical details makes it hard for me to understand this aspect of the system design and its importance to the overall effectiveness of the system.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "assignment mechanism detail"}, {"quote": "I don't fully agree with the authors' statement that non-coding tasks may introduce bias on page 7. Besides coding related tasks, many other tasks such as logic reasoning and mathematics analysis tasks may also be important for us to understand the effectiveness and broad usefulness of the newly developed multi-agent system.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "task selection rationale"}, {"quote": "I am not sure whether using success rate and human rating is sufficient and fair for comparing different approaches.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "evaluation metric sufficiency"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_xjornbs7aT_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_xjornbs7aT", "passages": [{"quote": "The motivation for this work is clear: it addresses the SCMDP problem by utilizing a model to learn a feasible action space, effectively converting SCMDP into an MDP and improving the algorithm's sample efficiency.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem motivation"}, {"quote": "The paper provides a step-by-step explanation of related concepts, making it very accessible to readers who may not be familiar with the field.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "accessibility"}, {"quote": "The description of the action and state spaces for the tasks in the experiments is clear, and the importance of the AM algorithm is effectively illustrated through visualizations at the end of the experimental section.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental clarity"}, {"quote": "The absence of accompanying code makes it difficult to replicate the experimental results.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "reproducibility"}, {"quote": "The experimental section appears somewhat limited, as it only tests the method in two environments. This reduces the persuasive power and credibility of the results.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental scope"}, {"quote": "The action mapping approach is not end-to-end, requiring pre-training with trajectory data before its application. This introduces additional costs, which are not adequately discussed in the paper.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "end-to-end limitation"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_xjornbs7aT_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_xjornbs7aT", "passages": [{"quote": "The most significant contribution is how action mapping allows agents to express multi-modal action distributions through a simple Gaussian in latent space, improving exploration.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "multi-modal distribution capability"}, {"quote": "Ability to plan with approximate feasibility models is a notable advantage since perfect models are rarely available in practical applications.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "approximate model utility"}, {"quote": "The paper omits constraint violation plots for the path planning task, making it impossible to verify claims about performance with approximate feasibility models.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "constraint violation reporting"}, {"quote": "The feasibility model is a critical component of the proposed architecture, yet the paper lacks essential analysis and ablations of this component. Key questions remain unanswered: How is state space sampling performed during training? What metrics determine sufficient training of the feasibility model? How does the quality/approximation level of the feasibility model impact overall performance?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "feasibility model analysis"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_xjornbs7aT_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_xjornbs7aT", "passages": [{"quote": "The method for training the feasibility policy is novel, allowing for fewer constraint violations and higher returns compared to other methods.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "training method novelty"}, {"quote": "Experiments were conducted in environments requiring constraints, such as a robotic arm task and a spline-based path planning.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "environment selection"}, {"quote": "The approach is straightforward and can be combined with any RL algorithm.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "generality"}, {"quote": "The assumption that the feasible policy can be pretrained seems overly strict. Pretraining requires prior knowledge of the cost function $C^\\tau(s;\\pi)$ and the feasibility model $G(s,a)$, which may be difficult to assume in general.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "pretraining assumption"}, {"quote": "The experimental environments appear limited. It would be beneficial to include comparisons in environments like Safety Gym or other constrained RL environments.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "environment diversity"}, {"quote": "There is a lack of baseline algorithms. Currently, the comparisons are limited to variants of action mapping, such as action resampling and projection. Direct comparisons with a wider range of methods, including Lagrangian approaches, would strengthen the evaluation.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline diversity"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_xjornbs7aT_r4", "paper_id": "iclr_xjornbs7aT", "passages": [{"quote": "The target problem to solve in this work is of great significance to many real-world applications.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem significance"}, {"quote": "The related works are introduced and discussed satisfactorily.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "related work discussion"}, {"quote": "Although the authors mentioned the reference [Theile et al., 2024], I found the content in Section 4 overlaps largely with the content in Section 3 and Section 4 of [Theile et al., 2024]. For example, Equation 16 in this paper is almost the same as the JS loss in Table 2 of [Theile et al., 2024]. Therefore, the novelty and contribution of this paper are questionable.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty and overlap"}, {"quote": "The whole training process is not clear. Adding a pseudocode of the proposed algorithm will help. Especially, the training of the feasibility model is not clear enough. In addition, many technical details are missing.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "training process clarity"}, {"quote": "The idea of action mapping is closely related to the research on action representation learning [1-4]. The illustration in Figure 1 is very similar to the concept presented by Figure 1 in [1]. These related works should be included in the related work section for a detailed discussion.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "related work completeness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_J1xtkJmFY3_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_J1xtkJmFY3", "passages": [{"quote": "This work tackles an interesting problem of enabling language models to reason about the modality of single cell genomics.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem interest"}, {"quote": "The experiments performed compare against supervised baselines.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "baseline comparison presence"}, {"quote": "There are many ablation studies performed to explore what aspects of the method are working.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "ablation study thoroughness"}, {"quote": "I expected to see more exploration into the method of incorporating a single cell observation. This work only discusses a single approach (besides the gene2vec ablation) while I'm sure the research team experienced many configurations that didn't work. It would be nice to have experiments with these alternatives as well.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "method exploration breadth"}, {"quote": "the method does not appear to be zero-shot due to pre-training for each task as confirmed by the statement \"To assess the performance in a zero-shot setting, SC datasets are split into a training set for pre-training and tuning, and a test set for evaluation\"", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "zero-shot claim validity"}, {"quote": "I now believe a re-framing of the paper should occur to prevent confusion. The method is not as simple as taking the SC sample embedding for a single cell and putting it in a prompt as the current framing implies.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "framing accuracy"}]}
